{"id":"n-20261010-dv7qy0","category":"health","display_title":"Terminal Cancer and Life Lessons","display_title_he":"סרטן סופני ולקחי חיים","short_summary":"A 65-year-old man living with terminal cancer shares how confronting childhood trauma and facing a terminal diagnosis taught him to appreciate the present moment.","english_level":"C1","words":453,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/New_Year%27s_Eve_Passion_Despite_it_All.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"See release, please. They are NOT mine. · CC BY-SA 4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Year%27s_Eve_Passion_Despite_it_All.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"BBC News","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8x2z22wdeglo","title":"'What terminal cancer has taught me about life'","published_at":"2026-10-10T06:16:46.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-10T06:16:46.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-10T12:52:23.556Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Facing a terminal illness can completely alter an individual's perspective on existence. For Rex Mackrill, a 65-year-old resident of Tetbury in Gloucestershire, being diagnosed with incurable stage four prostate cancer forced a profound reassessment of what truly matters. Because prostate cancer ran in his family, Rex regularly monitored his PSA levels. Following a raised test result and subsequent medical investigations, doctors confirmed his condition. Although he underwent chemotherapy, the physical toll proved significant, leaving him with enduring fatigue from hormone treatments and forcing him to step down from a career he loved as a general practice manager. Despite these immense challenges, his primary focus has remained firmly on his family, particularly his wife, Sarah, and their shared dedication to raising their boys.","Yet, the journey toward inner peace began years before his cancer diagnosis. For a large portion of his adult life, Rex experienced severe anxiety without understanding its origin. He often felt profoundly frightened while maintaining an outwardly calm exterior. The pivotal turning point occurred when he reached the age of 60, after a life coach observed that he tended to shut down during specific discussions. Rex ultimately realized this deep-rooted trauma stemmed from abuse suffered during his childhood. Research indicates that delayed post-traumatic stress disorder diagnoses are not uncommon, with symptoms frequently emerging years after the initial events. Approximately one in ten people in the UK are expected to experience PTSD during their lifetimes.","Seeking professional help proved transformative. Through Cognitive Behavioural Therapy and Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing therapy, the constant, overwhelming stress he carried gradually diminished. Looking back on his struggles, he advocates a straightforward approach for anyone facing similar internal blocks: reaching out to a trusted friend to find appropriate support. While coming to terms with terminal illness has brought practical changes—such as writing poetry, donating his car, and preparing for the future—it has also heightened his sense of presence. He no longer needs to travel to scenic locations like the Lake District to feel grounded; instead, he finds deep appreciation in simple, everyday occurrences, such as watching a bird navigate a windy tree branch.","As part of his journey, Rex has also engaged in planning sessions at Longfield Hospice in Minchinhampton, which assist patients and their families in discussing end-of-life care and wishes. Sarah Mimmack, the director of care at the hospice, noted that many families routinely avoid conversations about death due to discomfort, even though proactive planning can effectively remove uncertainty and alleviate pressure. For Rex, preparing for death has paradoxically become another meaningful way to embrace the present. Ultimately, his overarching message to others is to avoid waiting for a crisis to appreciate the beauty of the world and find joy in everyday life."],"facts":[{"fact":"Rex Mackrill, a 65-year-old man from Tetbury, Gloucestershire, is living with incurable stage four prostate cancer.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Rex regrets spending decades carrying unaddressed childhood trauma and anxiety without understanding its root.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"At age 60, a life coach noticed Rex would shut down during certain topics, leading him to discover that his trauma stemmed from childhood abuse.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Therapies like CBT and EMDR helped transform his life, reducing his constant anxiety.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Because of chemotherapy side effects and intense fatigue, Rex had to stop working as a GP practice manager.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Rex now focuses on the present moment, writes poetry, gives away possessions, and participates in end-of-life planning sessions at Longfield Hospice.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Facing","lemma":"face","pos":"verb","he":"התמודדות עם","name":false},{"text":"terminal","lemma":"terminal","pos":"adjective","he":"סופני","name":false},{"text":"illness","lemma":"illness","pos":"noun","he":"מחלה","name":false},{"text":"completely","lemma":"completely","pos":"adverb","he":"לחלוטין","name":false},{"text":"alter","lemma":"alter","pos":"verb","he":"לשנות","name":false},{"text":"individual","lemma":"individual","pos":"noun","he":"אדם","name":false},{"text":"perspective","lemma":"perspective","pos":"noun","he":"פרספקטיבה","name":false},{"text":"existence","lemma":"existence","pos":"noun","he":"קיום","name":false},{"text":"Rex Mackrill","lemma":"Rex Mackrill","pos":"name","he":"רקס מאקריל","name":true},{"text":"resident","lemma":"resident","pos":"noun","he":"תושב","name":false},{"text":"Tetbury","lemma":"Tetbury","pos":"name","he":"טטבורי","name":true